<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hanover</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Hannover</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hanover is a city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leine">Leine</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-saxony">Lower Saxony</a>. From 1636 it was the residence of the Guelph dukes of Calenberg, who became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a> of Hanover in 1692 and, from 1714, kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a>. It was the capital of the Kingdom of Hanover from 1814 until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> annexed it in 1866, and the philosopher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gottfried-wilhelm-leibniz">Leibniz</a> worked there as court librarian.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hanover.jpg" alt="Hanover" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: H. Helmlechner · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hannover_Blick_Neues_Rathaus_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The marriage of Frederick V to Elizabeth Stuart, daughter of James VI and I, in 1613 tied German Protestantism to Britain and the Dutch Republic, and through their daughter Sophia, electress of <b>Hanover</b>, the British throne passed to the house of Hanover in 1714.</blockquote><a href="/empire/electoral-palatinate">Electoral Palatinate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Johann Smidt, burgomaster for most of the period from 1821 to 1857, represented the city at the Congress and in 1827 bought land from <b>Hanover</b> at the mouth of the Weser to found Bremerhaven.</blockquote><a href="/empire/free-hanseatic-city-of-bremen">Free Hanseatic City of Bremen · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>…Congress of Vienna, joined 39 sovereign states and free cities: the Austrian Empire and the Kingdom of Prussia for their former imperial lands, kingdoms such as Bavaria, Saxony, Württemberg and <b>Hanover</b>, dozens of grand duchies, duchies and principalities, and the free cities of Frankfurt, Hamburg, Bremen and Lübeck.</blockquote><a href="/empire/german-confederation">German Confederation · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>On Queen Anne’s death in 1714 the crown passed under the Act of Settlement to George I, Elector of <b>Hanover</b>, and the Hanoverian kings, though still powerful in choosing ministers and directing foreign policy, needed majorities in the Commons.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-great-britain">Kingdom of Great Britain · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Seven Years’ War (1756–1763) Prussia, backed only by Britain and <b>Hanover</b>, fought Austria, France, Russia and Sweden, winning at Rossbach and Leuthen in 1757 but suffering crushing defeats such as Kunersdorf in 1759; it survived when Russia left the war after Empress Elizabeth died in 1762.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-prussia">Kingdom of Prussia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…Scania, though it was defeated at Helsingborg in 1710; the anti-Swedish coalition of the Great Northern War, which had faltered before Poltava, re-formed with renewed confidence; and over the following years Russia overran Livonia, Estonia and Finland while Sweden's German possessions fell to Denmark, Prussia, Saxony and <b>Hanover</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/swedish-empire">Swedish Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
